On trusting numbers
2026-11-28

I rented sixteen GPUs to run a language model on 128 fresh graphs. I had
a separate model grade its written reasoning, and I had confidence
intervals on the results. In retrospect, a good thing to have done
before renting the sixteen GPUs would have been to move answer A to
the bottom of the page.

<!--more-->

I was trying to find out how much you can learn about a language model
by reading the reasoning it writes before it gives you an answer.
The model I was using, VarLoop, has a convenient experimental feature.
I can take the same model weights and run part of the network several
times for each token it produces. I can also give it more tokens to
write out intermediate steps. These are two quite different ways of
spending compute. Only one of them gives me something to read.

Suppose I give the model a private instruction, and I can tell from
its answer whether the instruction had any effect. Can I dial up the
internal passes, dial down the written reasoning, and reach a point
where the model does the job just as well, still follows my instruction,
and gives a reader less evidence that it did so? The “just as well”
part matters. It's easy to get a model to say less about how it solved
a problem if you also make it incapable of solving the problem.

## A surprisingly popular letter

My first task involved following a path through a graph and picking
the endpoint from five alternatives. Multiple choice made the answers
cheap to score. It also gave the model two fairly good ideas about
what to output before it had followed a single edge. It could pick A,
or it could pick whatever appeared first. In a conventional list of
answers these are the same idea.

I eventually ran the cheap experiment. I took 24 questions, left the
graph and the letter assigned to each endpoint alone, and rearranged
the options. At five internal passes, with no written reasoning and
one letter allowed for the answer, the model picked A on 23 of the 24
questions in ordinary ABCDE order. Across the 96 rearrangements with A
somewhere else, it picked A 42 times and the first displayed letter 53
times. That leaves exactly one answer for which you have to entertain
a third explanation.

This diagnostic concerned a particular way of asking for an answer.
It was enough to make me withdraw the study. I had used performance on
that task to decide which two model settings were comparable. Every
subsequent comparison of their reasoning depended on that decision.

I tried asking for a three-digit endpoint instead. Of 1,536 baseline
answers during development, 1,054 failed the required format. In 258
of those failures I could find the correct traversal in the response.
I had got rid of A. Now I had a graph benchmark that awarded zero for
some demonstrably correct graph work.

## Four tokens for free

I switched to GraphWalks. A question gives you a list of directed edges,
a starting node and a distance. Imagine that A points to B and C, B
points to D, and C points to E. The nodes at distance two from A are D
and E. I can check that answer with a few lines of ordinary breadth-first
search. I can also privately ask the model to put E first. Both orders
get exactly the same graph score. Reversing the instruction on the same
graph lets me see whether it actually influenced the answer.

This time I kept two versions of the measurement. In one, the model
had to produce the whole answer in the benchmark's format. In the
other, I supplied the opening four tokens, `Final Answer: [`, and let
it take things from there. The opening contains no node IDs. On the
same 67 graphs, with zero tokens allocated to the scratchpad, the ordinary
version produced just four parseable answers in 268 attempts. Giving
the model the opening brought its average score at one pass from 1.5%
to 19.7%. That's node-set F1, which gives partial credit for getting
some of the right nodes.

I went back and read what the model had actually written. At one pass,
66 of the 67 ordinary answers started with “We need”. One began by
saying it needed to reason step by step, copied out edges, and ran out
of tokens halfway through a node ID. Turning off the scratchpad still
left the model free to do its working in the answer box. My scorer
looked for a completed list on the last line. Only one answer qualified.

Starting the answer with an open bracket gave the model a different
sentence to finish. This time 52 of the 67 answers parsed and ten had
exactly the right nodes. Had the model known the answers all along? In
five ordinary responses I could find the correct nonempty set somewhere
in brackets, though an intermediate step can contain the right nodes by
chance. On the graph where it was cut off mid-ID, I have no idea what
it would eventually have answered. What I can see is why the score went
up: the model started giving me node IDs while it still had room.

The next result was the one that made me want to rent more GPUs. With
512 tokens to write things down, the average scores at 1, 3, 5 and 10
passes were 26.6%, 50.2%, 53.2% and 54.7%. At 128 and 256 tokens they
also rose at every step. I drew curves through the grid to show where
you might trade written reasoning for more internal passes at the same
score. The curves looked beautifully smooth. There were four measured
depths. My plotting code supplied everything in between.

The zero-token measurements had been sitting in that same table all
along: 19.7%, 18.4%, 17.7%, 20.6%. That row gave me considerably less
reason to believe I could keep taking away the scratchpad.

## Does the same thing happen with Dory?

I also tried Dory, NVIDIA's four-billion-parameter looped model. When a
setting appears to do very little, it's worth checking whether you
actually turned it on. Dory lets you set `n_recurrent_loops` at inference
time on the same weights. I instrumented the loop in the serving code
and checked the recorded iteration counts, including 8, 16 and 32.
Those last three go beyond the one to four loops in NVIDIA's published
comparison.

Dory did show some benefit on my graphs. With the list opening and
512 reasoning tokens, its mean F1 on four development graphs went from
25.0% at one pass to 39.3% at four. The result that looked flat was
further out. On 17 matched graphs at the same 512-token budget, going
from 8 to 16 passes cost 2.9 F1 points on average. Going from 16 to
32 gained zero. The uncertainty on that last difference ran from
−28.8 to +28.8 points (95% interval). VarLoop also flattened out in
that high-pass comparison. The convincing gain I saw for VarLoop was
earlier, especially from one to three passes on the 67-graph grid.

NVIDIA recommends starting Dory at two loops. On SWE-bench Verified,
where the model fixes bugs in software repositories, NVIDIA reports
50.3% at one loop, 59.5% at two and 61.8% at four. On GPQA Diamond,
a science question-answering benchmark, the scores were 69.19%,
72.41% and 72.16%. I'd happily spend the compute for a second loop
on the strength of those numbers. My graph experiment gave me little
reason to pay for a thirty-second. [NVIDIA's model card and benchmark results](https://huggingface.co/nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-Labs-Dory1.0-4B-BF16#benchmarks)

## How many graphs is 100%?

I extended the grid to 128 passes and 6,144 reasoning tokens. Running
every setting on every graph was expensive, so I started with small
samples. At 128 passes and a 256-token allowance I got 100%. The model
had seen one graph. After seven distinct graphs the score was 36.7%,
with two exactly right. Across the development grid, 46 settings
initially solved everything I had given them. Once I supplied more
graphs, 25 of those settings made a mistake.

I followed up the zero-token row on all 67 original graphs. That meant
469 new answers across seven additional depths, for $31.21 in GPU time.
Only 16 passes cleared my 25% target, at 26.6%. At 128 passes the
score was 23.9%. Even at the best-looking depth, the 95% interval
from resampling graphs ran from 16.4% to 37.3%. I put `n = 67` under
every entry in that column. Some of the cells immediately to its right
still have `n = 1`. You ought to be able to see that without reading
the methods section.

Then I wondered what GPUs I'd actually rented. The original shallow
grid ran on Server Edition cards. The deeper run mixed server and
workstation cards, with different power limits. Fortunately I had kept
the worker records, and two workers had sometimes picked up the same
job. Across 22 repeated graph-and-depth combinations the F1 scores
agreed every time, even though the answer text differed on 12. On 20
of those repeats, the median ratio of workstation time to server time
was 0.34. I'd be wary of quoting a speedup from two rows of that table
without checking which machine ran them.

There was also something the logs let me keep. On 66 of the 67 graphs,
the very same worker had run both 16 and 24 passes. Restricting the
comparison to those graphs still gave a drop from 27.0% to 19.4%.
Whatever made that particular dip, shifting the work from one kind of
GPU to another doesn't explain it.

## The experiment I didn't run

The point of all this was to compare what a reader could detect at
equal task performance. The curve suggested four settings near 25% F1.
Before paying for a larger test of the private ordering instruction, I
tried the settings on three shared graphs with multiple correct answers.
At the reference setting, ten passes and 34 reasoning tokens, the mean
score was zero. I'd set an acceptance band of 20–30%. I stopped there.

Three graphs can't tell me exactly where that curve goes. They can
tell me that the comparison I was about to buy hadn't met my own
condition for buying it. That's probably the decision from this project
I'm happiest with. I still have the responses, the worker IDs and the
scores if I want to try a different comparison. And if I ever run the
multiple-choice version again, shuffling the options is going in the
first batch.
